TypeScript is a programming language that was developed by Microsoft in 2011. It is an open-source, superset of JavaScript that provides a more concise syntax for writing code.

Duck typing is a feature of TypeScript that allows developers to specify the type of an object at runtime without having to explicitly declare it. This means that if you have a function that accepts an object as its parameter, and you want to ensure that the object passed to it has a specific type, you can simply use the `typeof` operator to check the type of the object:

```
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

const result = add(5, 'hello');
console.log(typeof result); // "number"
```

In this example, we are adding two numbers together using the `add()` function. We then print out the type of the resulting value using the `typeof` operator. Since the `result` variable is of type `number`, we see that the `add()` function expects a number as its argument.

Typescript also supports implicit typing, which means that TypeScript will automatically infer the type of an object based on its properties and methods. For example, if we define a class with only one property called `name`, TypeScript will automatically infer that the class should be of type string:

```
class Person {
  name: string;
}

const person = new Person();
person.name = 'John';
console.log(person.name); // "John"
```

In this example, we create a new instance of the `Person` class and assign it to the `person` variable. We then set the `name` property of the `person` object to the string "John". Finally, we log the value of the `name` property to the console, which outputs "John" since the `Person` class is inferred to be of type string.

